{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791593733,"nonce":"554nSevZayDPvDTqMN/Rsqc+UsY=","message":"Within-call reuse landed in 5b7a89a5bb33b88b2c27447fe98cc339e91efe26: each item's inference records what it read (return types and parameter types per item, class fields per class and name, closures, list facts, list-field kinds, dynamic fields and per-class field types per name, shape classes), and a round keeps an item's result while none of those changed since. ZYNTAX_CHECK_INFER_DEPS=1 re-infers every kept item and panics where they disagree; it passed on all 22 speed kernels' start-up and the whole zyntax_python test suite (50/50 conformance).\n\nKept inferences (ZYNTAX_TRACE_TYPES_ROUNDS): go 105-119 of ~230 per call (the probe's ceiling was 59%), richards 170/298, deltablue 184/331, scimark_fft 99/205, raytrace 86-97/233, nbody 22/62.\n\nStart-up instructions against 80fc7099 (run stopped once start-up is done, ZYPY_PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3, Apple M-series): total of 22 kernels 150.98G -\u003e 141.48G (-6.3%); go -12.1%, richards -14.0%, deltablue -9.3%, scimark_* -7.3%, raytrace -5.8%, nbody -5.3%.\n\nStill open here: reuse across infer_module calls. Each call rebuilds the module, so its first inner round infers every item again, and the call after a class gains fields reproduced the previous call in every kernel probed. Keeping those would need each read's value (not a change time) recorded, and the item's effects (return type, list facts, field widenings, argument joins, closure updates, record writes) replayed into the fresh module.","files":null}]}